---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
---
<Base title="About">
  <ol class="crumbs"><li><a href={href('/')}>All topics</a></li><li>About</li></ol>
  <h1 class="page-title">About these articles</h1>
  <p class="lede">A growing set of articles that explain one piece of a robot at a time, in the order you would need them to understand a real machine.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="max-width: var(--measure); margin-top: 2rem">
    <h2>How to use the site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Follow the outline.</strong> The sidebar lists every topic and article. Within a topic, articles build on each other in order.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick your level.</strong> Each article is marked intro, intermediate or advanced. The home page can filter by level.</li>
      <li><strong>Track what you have read.</strong> Mark an article as read and it is ticked in the outline. Progress is stored in your browser only, so it does not follow you to another device.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>How the articles are written</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Reasoning first.</strong> Each article explains why a design exists and what it trades away, before any catalogue of parts.</li>
      <li><strong>Sourced.</strong> Claims about a specific product, company or paper link to where they came from. Vendor-reported numbers are labelled as vendor-reported.</li>
      <li><strong>Dated.</strong> Robotics moves quickly. Every article shows when it was last updated, and "recent" always means "as of that date".</li>
      <li><strong>Typical, not exact.</strong> Ranges such as gear ratios and efficiencies are typical engineering values. Worked examples use round, invented numbers and say so. Check a datasheet before designing around them.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>If something is wrong or out of date, open an issue on the <a href="https://github.com/TechBOiz/robotics-learn">GitHub repository</a> with the source that shows the correct information.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
